          "question": "I am 68 with COPD and I have been more breathless for three days with green sputum. Is this an emergency, and what does guidance describe about exacerbations?",
          "answer": "**Based on what you've described, I can't say whether this is an emergency, and you should not wait to find out.** The risk check did not match any emergency pattern, but it says that is not a clearance. Please contact your doctor or a COPD nurse today. If you have any of the signs below, call emergency services now.\n\n**Call emergency services immediately if you have:**\n- Breathlessness at rest, or breathlessness that came on suddenly.\n- Trouble speaking in full sentences, or having to pause for breath between words.\n- Blue or grey lips, face or fingertips.\n- Drowsiness, confusion or unusual sleepiness. The COPD guidance says this can signal a dangerous rise in carbon dioxide, not just tiredness. (Source: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) summary; Severe difficulty breathing guidance)\n- Chest pain, or coughing up blood.\n\nThe COPD summary also says worsening breathlessness that does not respond to your usual reliever inhaler needs urgent assessment, not a routine appointment. If that describes you, get urgent help.\n\n**What the guidance says about exacerbations:** The COPD summary describes a flare-up as a worsening of symptoms that needs urgent assessment. It also recommends having a written plan for what to do during one, which your clinician may have already given you. Your three days of worse breathlessness with green sputum is the kind of change that plan is meant for, so check it now.\n\n**What the retrieved material does not cover:** The sources I found do not say what green sputum means in someone with COPD, or whether it points to an infection. The antibiotic guidance I found is about upper respiratory infections in general and does not apply to your situation, so please don't rely on it. Any decision about treatment needs to come from your clinician, who can examine you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
